X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Sebastopol Center for the Arts": "https://www.sebarts.org/classes-lectures/experimental-paper-sculpture-2026"}
X-SOURCE-ID:sebarts
SUMMARY:Experimental Paper Sculpture: 4/18-4/19 — Sebastopol Center For 
 the Arts\, California
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260418T000000
DTEND;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260419T000000
UID:0b813710916233abd35cefcd50512caa@sebarts.org
DESCRIPTION:Experimental Paper Sculpture 2026\n\n  \n    \n      \n       
  \n          $195.00\n        \n      \n    \n  \n\nSaturday & Sunday\, Ap
 ril 18-19\, 2026\; 10am-4pm$165 + $30 Materials FeeFor any student interes
 ted in using handmade paper to create sculptural forms with armatures\, th
 is is a class for you! Students will learn building methods for armatures 
 and ways to wrap and dip their armatures in handmade paper prepared in a w
 ay that is called "high shrinkage". In this method\, paper pulp has been b
 eaten for a long period of time to absorb a large amount of water\, and dr
 amatic transformations can occur when the form dries.Papermaking is a wet 
 process\, please wear clothing and shoes that can get wet. Papermaking als
 o involves some lifting and arm movements.About the Instructor: Michelle W
 ilson is an artist living in Oakland CA\, whose work explores hand paperma
 king traditions from all over the world. Her practice includes frequent co
 llaborations with other artists\; in particular the Rhinoceros Project (wi
 th Anne Beck). She has been making paper since 2002. Wilson has an MFA in 
 Book Arts and Printmaking from the University of the Arts\, and a BFA from
  Moore College of Art and Design. She lectures and teaches widely\, includ
 ing places such as Haystack Mountain School of Crafts\, Magnolia Editions\
 , the San Jose Institute of Contemporary Art Print Center\, the Kala Art I
 nstitute\, and currently teaches papermaking\, printmaking\, and book arts
  at Stanford University.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n
 \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n          \n            \n              \n                
 Add to cart
LOCATION:Sebastopol Center for the Arts\, 282 S High St\, Sebastopol\, CA 
 95472
URL:https://www.sebarts.org/classes-lectures/experimental-paper-sculpture-
 2026
X-SOURCE:Sebastopol Center for the Arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Sebastopol Center for the Arts": "https://www.sebarts.org/classes-lectures/pt-2-glaze-as-a-completion-of-form-with-ping-zhong"}
X-SOURCE-ID:sebarts
SUMMARY:Pt 2: Glaze as a Completion of Form with Ping Zhong — Sebastopol
  Center For the Arts\, California
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260418T000000
DTEND;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260419T000000
UID:142952735a64cee5d77faa4e9354bc36@sebarts.org
DESCRIPTION:Pt 2: Glaze as a Completion of Form with Ping Zhong\n\n  \n   
  \n      \n        \n          $10.00\n        \n      \n    \n  \n\nSatur
 day\, April 18\, 2026\; 10am-11amLocated in the Ceramics StudioThis sessio
 n will explore how glaze can enhance and finalize a piece. SebArts’ Cera
 mics Residency Artist\, Ping Zhong\, plans to spend the first month testin
 g the studio’s existing glazes to understand their behavior\, and will a
 lso bring a few personal glaze recipes to share\, along with fired samples
 . Application methods and techniques for achieving specific effects will b
 e demonstrated. The instructor will have works ready for glazing\, and par
 ticipants from the previous workshop may also have pieces they wish to gla
 ze.Due to limited capacity of the Ceramics Studio\, we are asking for a su
 ggested donation of $10 to confirm your attendance. About the Artist: Ping
  Zhong’s artistic practice is a continuous exploration of regeneration
 —of materials\, forms\, and cultural narratives. She investigates the di
 alogue between ancient Chinese pottery and contemporary visual language\, 
 using wheel-throwing\, hand-building\, and sculpture to create works in wh
 ich history and personal growth converge. This reinterpretation of classic
 al forms is not only an artistic method\, but also a philosophical inquiry
  into the harmony between past and future. Deeply motivated to uncover the
  potential within clay\, Ping pushes the boundaries of design and techniqu
 e. Her work reflects on universal themes: the individual’s place within 
 the community\, the relationship between essence and appearance\, and the 
 evolving connection between nature and society. Through these explorations
 \, she seeks to create art that resonates across cultures.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n
 \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n          \n            
 \n              \n                Add to cart
LOCATION:Sebastopol Center for the Arts\, 282 S High St\, Sebastopol\, CA 
 95472
URL:https://www.sebarts.org/classes-lectures/pt-2-glaze-as-a-completion-of
 -form-with-ping-zhong
X-SOURCE:Sebastopol Center for the Arts
END:VEVENT
